      <h1>Heijōshin (平常心)</h1>
      <p class="concept-hero-subtitle">Ordinary practice is no ready-made answer.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="news-meta">October 12, 2026</p>

    <p>
      Does “ordinary mind” mean simply doing what one usually does? If so, why
      practise attention and accept correction? And if not, does heijōshin become
      another image of an ideally calm self? That tension matters more than a
      promise that nothing will unsettle us on a difficult day.
    </p>
    <p>
      In everyday Japanese, <strong>heijōshin</strong> means composure or presence
      of mind; dictionaries also record the Buddhist <em>byōjōshin</em>, “ordinary
      mind” or ordinary feelings and way of life.<sup><a href="#heijoshin-source-1">[1]</a></sup><sup><a href="#heijoshin-source-2">[2]</a></sup>
      Their shared written form does not make these usages interchangeable. Case 19
      of <em>Wumen’s Gate</em> shows why the ordinary mind in this Chan dialogue
      cannot be reduced to a calm mood.
    </p>

    <h2>Neither habit nor special state</h2>
    <p>
      Zhaozhou asks Nanquan about the Path and hears: “The ordinary mind is the
      Path.” When he asks whether one can go toward it, Nanquan replies that
      going toward it takes one away. Zhaozhou does not stop there: he asks how
      to recognise the Path without trying. The reply rejects both knowing,
      which becomes false awareness, and not-knowing, which is oblivion. Wumen
      adds that even after awakening Zhaozhou still had thirty more years of
      study ahead.<sup><a href="#heijoshin-source-3">[3]</a></sup>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is neither praise of autopilot nor advice to “stop trying”. It is not
      an instruction for manufacturing calm. Hershock describes Chan teaching as
      responsive to particular situations, rather than a set of universal
      formulas.<sup><a href="#heijoshin-source-4">[4]</a></sup> For a contemporary
      Aikido teacher, this can prompt a question: is the student responding to
      what is happening now, or merely repeating a familiar movement—or trying
      to look untouched? This is a modern teaching analogy, not evidence of a
      historical Zen connection to a particular training method.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ordinary practice matters: it builds familiarity with form, ukemi and safe
      ways of working. But it is no ready-made answer to every situation. In a
      regular class, nage may notice that a partner is not ready, stop and check
      what is happening; uke may say that the contact or pace makes it unsafe to
      continue.
    </p>
    <p>
      After a teacher’s correction, the student need neither defend the old
      version nor copy the new one mindlessly: try it, check the effect with the
      partner, and ask if the guidance is unclear. The teacher watches the choice
      and quality of contact, not a serene face. Either person can slow down or
      stop; an unagreed attack is not an exercise in attention.
    </p>

    <h2>Practice continues in our relationships</h2>
    <p>
      A similar tension arises at work when we find an error in information we
      have shared. We can focus on how we look, or check who has already acted
      on the mistake, name it clearly and correct the information. Composure
      does not erase consequences or responsibility; repair may require a
      conversation and accepting that the other person is angry or does not
      immediately trust us.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same principle applies at home, but a family member is not an uke to
      be “guided”. If we answer a child unfairly or dismiss a partner, returning
      our attention begins with acknowledging what we did, listening to the
      response and repairing what we can. It does not mean demanding agreement
      or instant forgiveness. Composure is not obedience, suppressed disagreement or
      tolerating harm; sometimes a responsible response is to set a boundary,
      leave or seek help.
    </p>
    <p>
      In both settings, preparation and habits help, but do not release us from
      noticing another person or checking the effects of our actions. This is a
      modest transfer of a question from the dojo, not a technique for controlling
      relationships, therapy or a guaranteed result. Practice may help us receive
      correction; it does not prove that someone is calm or right.
    </p>
    <p>
      So return to the ordinary, not to an ideal image of yourself: a form we
      have learned is the beginning of action, not its automatic conclusion. A
      partner may change what needs to be done; another person’s response may
      show that our first judgement was wrong. Ordinary mind does not exempt us
      from further learning—Wumen leaves Zhaozhou thirty more years—and that is
      why the next movement must be checked in the world, not only in our image
      of ourselves.
    </p>

